 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Sensing element",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "A surface-micromachined silicon proof mass hangs from a few anchor points and moves along the measured axis. Its displacement unbalances a capacitive half-bridge; the rest capacitances are a few pF and full-scale motion changes them by femtofarads. A cap over the element keeps the mould compound off the moving structure."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Measurement chain",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "A low-noise charge amplifier turns the capacitance imbalance into a voltage, and an A/D converter turns that into a number. Data are read over I2C or SPI, and a data-ready signal flags each new X, Y, Z set."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Factory calibration",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "Sensitivity and zero-g level are trimmed at the factory at 2.5 V. The trim lives in non-volatile memory and is copied into registers at every power-up, so no user calibration is needed. Boot takes about 5 ms after power-up."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supplies",
   "page": 20,
   "text": "Vdd powers the core and Vdd_IO the I/O pads. Decoupling of 100 nF ceramic plus 10 µF aluminium belongs as close to pin 8 as possible. All supplies and grounds should come up together. Vdd may be removed with Vdd_IO kept: the bus stays usable and the measurement chain is off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Operating modes",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Low-power mode gives 8-bit data, normal mode 10-bit and high-resolution mode 12-bit, chosen by LPen in CTRL_REG1 and HR in CTRL_REG4; both set at once is not allowed. Bandwidth is ODR/2 in the first two and ODR/9 in high resolution. Changing into 12-bit mode takes 7/ODR; other changes take 1/ODR."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Self-test",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Self-test applies an electrostatic force to the proof mass that stands in for a known acceleration, so the output shifts by an amount set by full scale and sensitivity. The output is the sum of real acceleration and the test force; a shift inside the Vst limits shows the sensor and its interface are working."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Zero-g level",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "With no acceleration the output should be mid-scale, 00h in two's complement; a part lying flat reads 0 g on X and Y and 1 g on Z. Mounting stress on the board can move the offset slightly; it moves little with temperature."
  },
  {
   "topic": "6D / 4D orientation",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "The interrupt generators can flag when the part rests in a known orientation. In 4D mode the Z axis is left out of the position detection."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sleep to wake and return to sleep",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "With a threshold in Act_THS (3Eh) and a duration in Act_DUR (3Fh), the part drops to low-power mode at 10 Hz ODR while acceleration stays under the threshold, ignoring the ODR, LPen and HR settings. When acceleration rises above the threshold, the programmed mode and data rate return."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interrupts",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "Two independent generators produce wake-up and free-fall events on the enabled axes, and both can be active at once on the two pins. Thresholds, timing and routing of INT1 and INT2 are all set over the serial interface. Reading INT1_SRC or INT2_SRC clears its IA bit and the pin, and refreshes the register when latching is on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "FIFO",
   "page": 21,
   "text": "Each axis has a 32-slot, 10-bit buffer, set by FM bits in FIFO_CTRL_REG (2Eh). Bypass: the buffer is unused and only the first slot carries data. FIFO mode: data fill the buffer, then collection stops when it is full. Stream: once full, the oldest sample is dropped for each new one. Stream-to-FIFO: runs as stream until a trigger event, then as FIFO. A watermark level can raise an interrupt; watermark, empty and full events can go to INT1 or INT2."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reading the FIFO",
   "page": 21,
   "text": "Reads of OUT_X, OUT_Y and OUT_Z (29h, 2Bh, 2Dh) return the oldest stored samples in stream, trigger or FIFO mode; single and burst reads both work. A burst read wraps from 2Dh back to 28h, so emptying all 32 levels takes 192 bytes (six registers times 32)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature sensor",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "Set TEMP_EN in TEMP_CFG_REG to turn it on, and BDU in CTRL_REG4 to 1 to read it. Read both OUT_TEMP_L and OUT_TEMP_H; the result sits in OUT_TEMP_H as 8-bit two's complement, left justified, at 1 digit/°C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output data format",
   "page": 37,
   "text": "Each axis output is a 16-bit two's complement word across an _L and _H register pair, left justified; the number of valid bits follows the operating mode."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interface selection",
   "page": 23,
   "text": "I2C and SPI share the same pads. CS high selects I2C (connect it to Vdd_IO); CS low selects SPI. SPI runs in 4-wire mode by default and in 3-wire mode when SIM in CTRL_REG4 is set."
  },
  {
   "topic": "I2C",
   "page": 24,
   "text": "The device is a slave with 7-bit address 001100x; SDO/SA0 sets the last bit, 0011000b when tied to ground and 0011001b when tied to supply, so two parts can share one bus. SCL and SDA each need a pull-up to Vdd_IO. Standard and fast (400 kHz) modes are supported. After the address comes an 8-bit sub-address: the lower seven bits pick the register and the top bit turns on auto-increment for multi-byte transfers. A read uses a repeated start after the sub-address. Data go MSb first. A receiver that cannot yet take another byte may hold SCL low to make the transmitter wait."
  },
  {
   "topic": "SPI",
   "page": 26,
   "text": "CS low frames a transfer; SPC idles high while CS is high. Data change on the falling edge of SPC and are sampled on the rising edge. A single-register access is 16 clocks: bit 0 is RW (1 read, 0 write), bit 1 is MS (1 auto-increments the address on multi-byte access), bits 2-7 are the address AD5-AD0, and bits 8-15 the data, MSb first. In a read the part drives SDO from bit 8. Further bytes add 8 clocks each. In 3-wire mode the data come back on SDA/SDI/SDO, and multi-byte reads work there too."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 20,
   "text": "The LGA package meets ECOPACK, RoHS and Green requirements and is qualified for soldering heat to JEDEC J-STD-020. The pin 1 indicator pad is left unconnected."
  }
 ],
